ains 6 random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive), call the ","key":"w9KERbnDXn"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set_seed()","key":"jc6WIuKtKb"},{"type":"text","value":" function and use ","key":"FtvCitiw98"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"42","key":"BosJWsN5oq"},{"type":"text","value":" as the seed parameter.","key":"xxEW6YvYM3"}],"key":"dc2nsod4fS"}],"key":"ffnbOpE44R"},{"type":"break","key":"LzQtfzTY8S"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Create a tensor “x” from the array above","key":"pS5SkNEYIV"}],"key":"axsc7Nbb43"}],"key":"J1049wK1W0"},{"type":"break","key":"cFPGaZHAq9"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Change the dtype of x from ","key":"zaUzRvTdr2"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int32","key":"GYGZVHSt1f"},{"type":"text","value":" to ","key":"LoNLJYFQqW"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int64","key":"LnaDBsglGZ"}],"key":"mxDGAzKznP"}],"key":"dLaiC7MQ6y"},{"type":"break","key":"eRRcrgbkLR"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Reshape ","key":"gpwHDYvbM3"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"uygS24YqVX"},{"type":"text","value":" into a 3x2 tensor","key":"MoZOfScIv3"}],"key":"DWuFOtC9na"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"SqYb9BXtvp"},{"type":"break","key":"UVZLU0MZK7"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"LAha1T0fyz"}],"key":"AKgNFa3zve"},{"type":"break","key":"t0SKBMJ7r6"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Return the right-hand column of tensor ","key":"q4bPHhKuGL"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"c3qAonBuMP"}],"key":"KEYRmbSdLO"}],"key":"bKFtjyVv4V"},{"type":"break","key":"aBEYLSF4F2"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of ","key":"hIawIHhdLa"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"jRDED8P82z"}],"key":"g4Q7SRTPPF"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"BqZg3pPArR"},{"type":"break","key":"syD5Z03eo2"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"QSr2uzYi68"}],"key":"br6OwucFIN"},{"type":"break","key":"GkV6xgTfz4"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9. Create a tensor ","key":"XyAwzYyGe8"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"w4Q90jXKu9"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same number of elements as ","key":"WU6qqHQxbq"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"RdL2BIhkcM"},{"type":"text","value":", that can be matrix-multiplied with ","key":"Uo2LTuZLiY"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"vWCTjjm3ud"}],"key":"bOymlt6gF3"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"oI0InGhBzQ"},{"type":"break","key":"TleMjXBoSy"},{"type":"text","value":"Use PyTorch directly (not NumPy) to create a tensor of random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive). Use 42 as seed. ","key":"gDehhfzccs"},{"type":"break","key":"ZINc3H3svA"},{"type":"text","value":"Think about what shape it should have to permit matrix multiplication.","key":"frAFfE1Bj0"}],"key":"juNlgeUAnM"},{"type":"break","key":"Rk0j4b3a0e"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10. Find the matrix product of ","key":"a3tKK047uj"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"E3xHegsUN6"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","key":"juL7y71DSq"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"zyzXbZy0eA"},{"type":"text","value":".","key":"xCJNHLB3H5"}],"key":"uibUhgi746"}],"key":"KDx7NKH8x3"}],"key":"T7vVYwqw8q"}],"key":"zFJ2IddjNa"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"hpnfiTSSVR"}],"identifier":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","label":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","html_id":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","implicit":true,"key":"s6I883ilLT"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This section sets up the foundational libraries required for tensor operations and mathematical computations. The set_seed() function is defined to ensure reproducible results across both NumPy and PyTorch environments by fixing the random number generators using torch.manual_seed(). This guarantees that “random” tensor generation will yield the exact same values every time the code runs.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"jdCcdZQCEE"}],"key":"LaEk9usaIb"}],"key":"xiRBV9QArZ"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 1. Perform Standard Imports\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\n# 2. Create a function called set_seed()\ndef set_seed(seed: int):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)","key":"f9WgqnR9Bh"},{"type":"outputs","id":"GzzDDl5bKB5EpLgfSeCEq","children":[],"key":"bezoGVb9j6"}],"key":"hRK4ii3BxG"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"ndSvbod4zl"}],"identifier":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","label":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","html_id":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","implicit":true,"key":"nXFxJiQqut"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, a NumPy array of 6 random integers is generated and immediately converted into a PyTorch tensor using torch.from_numpy(). Because PyTorch operations strictly require matching datatypes, the .type() method is explicitly used to cast the tensor from int32 to a 64-bit integer (torch.int64). Finally, the flat tensor is transformed into a 2D matrix (3 rows and 2 columns) using .reshape(), which safely alters the tensor’s view without modifying the original memory block.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"k1l07agBc3"}],"key":"uD1CXMna9l"}],"key":"suaMDoHd0h"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 3. Create a NumPy array \nset_seed(42)\narr = np.random.randint(0, 5, 6)\n\n# 4. Create a tensor \"x\" from the array above\nx = torch.from_numpy(arr)\n\n# 5. Change the dtype of x from int32 to int64\nx = x.type(torch.int64)\n\n# 6. Reshape x into a 3x2 tensor\nx = x.reshape(3, 2)","key":"Zf5D6Vw9IP"},{"type":"outputs","id":"VpIWw6pr2me5nETngtO45","children":[],"key":"hfNqp0r8aN"}],"key":"nR5caU2uge"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"gfVLFxvlOS"}],"identifier":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","label":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","html_id":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","implicit":true,"key":"jyDddI4DC1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This block demonstrates PyTorch’s native data manipulation capabilities. Extracting specific values from a tensor uses the exact same slicing syntax as NumPy arrays. The slice [:, 1:] grabs all rows (:) but only the elements from index 1 onwards (1:), effectively returning the right-hand column as a 2D slice. The .pow(2) function performs an element-wise arithmetic power operation, returning a brand new tensor containing the squared values without altering the original tensor x.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"mOztsaxPgT"}],"key":"GOqn10LUxI"}],"key":"hWh9pEQjQx"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 7. Return the right-hand column of tensor x\nright_column = x[:, 1:] \n\n# 8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of x\nx_squared = x.pow(2)","key":"WF8RFspgFV"},{"type":"outputs","id":"CBIU0o7YnfDGogZhaPxgf","children":[],"key":"ymLH6uY1sP"}],"key":"OXapfKCfzi"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"YTzg6XITZb"}],"identifier":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","label":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","html_id":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","implicit":true,"key":"rsrnJJ0hOU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To perform a 2D matrix multiplication, the number of columns in the first tensor must perfectly match the number of rows in the second tensor. Since tensor x has the shape (3, 2), the new tensor y is explicitly created from scratch using torch.randint() with an inverted shape of (2, 3). The operation ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"ANGOkwppiy"},{"type":"link","url":"http://torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"NvVLZIbpiT"}],"urlSource":"http://torch.mm","key":"SNFnEuCHyP"},{"type":"text","value":"(x, y) then calculates the matrix product.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"JX68pfZ2uF"}],"key":"zxoWDB2RCQ"}],"key":"WOOHYUJEe6"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 9. Create a tensor y with the same number of elements as x, that can be matrix-multiplied with x\nset_seed(42)\ny = torch.randint(0, 5, (2, 3))\n\n# 10. Find the matrix product of x and y\nmatrix_prod = torch.mm(x, y) \n\n# Print statements to verify the outputs\nprint(\"x:\\n\", x)\nprint(\"\\nright_column:\\n\", right_column)\nprint(\"\\nx_squared:\\n\", x_squared)\nprint(\"\\ny:\\n\", y)\nprint(\"\\nMatrix Product (x * y):\\n\", matrix_prod)","key":"t1HuMKD4yv"},{"type":"outputs","id":"sKSBgrmCq0kbthqPAt9JK","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"x:\n tensor([[3, 4],\n        [2, 4],\n        [4, 1]])\n\nright_column:\n tensor([[4],\n        [4],\n        [1]])\n\nx_squared:\n tensor([[ 9, 16],\n        [ 4, 16],\n        [16,  1]])\n\ny:\n tensor([[2, 2, 1],\n        [4, 1, 0]])\n\nMatrix Product (x * y):\n tensor([[22, 10,  3],\n        [20,  8,  2],\n        [12,  9,  4]])\n"},"children":[],"key":"cE9rkMVjIo"}],"key":"k2zYnBCm0b"}],"key":"kKaK1GRjJk"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tensor Reshaping and Slicing Verification","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"MkMOuUsGho"}],"key":"FwfVrSo1pf"}],"key":"DsohIdQz1c"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The printed output of x confirms it was successfully reshaped into a 3x2 configuration. The right_column output [[4], [4], [1]] proves that the slicing operation [:, 1:] successfully extracted the final column while maintaining its proper 2D column-vector shape rather than flattening it into a 1D array.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"t3BEJ8oiO7"}],"key":"atajf55msV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Element-Wise Power Dynamics","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"jy8BcqfnBD"}],"key":"PfZebOQu24"},{"type":"text","value":"\nThe x_squared output directly mirrors the shape of x, proving that basic arithmetic operations like pow() are applied on an element-by-element basis. Every individual scalar inside the matrix was multiplied by itself (e.g., the 3 became 9, the 4 became 16), entirely independently of the other elements in the tensor.   Dimensionality and the Matrix Product\nThe final output perfectly demonstrates the mathematical laws of matrix multiplication. Tensor x has a size of (3, 2) and tensor y has a size of (2, 3). Because their inner dimensions match (2 columns and 2 rows), the multiplication is valid. The resulting tensor natively adopts the outer dimensions of the two parent matrices, successfully outputting a new tensor with the expected shape of (3, 3).","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"sMUoxAu7zF"}],"key":"AWCmdk1nJs"}],"key":"JpixM4mMv9"}],"key":"r5eODy6xvo"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Laboratory Task 4 (01_Understanding Deep Learning)","short_title":"Task 4: Understanding Deep Learning","url":"/laboratory-task-4-01-understanding-deep-learning","group":"Personal Portfolio"},"next":{"title":"Laboratory   Task  6 ( E1    CNN  Implementation )","short_title":"Task 6: CNN Implementation","url":"/laboratory-task-6-e1-cnn-implementation","group":"Personal Portfolio"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}